---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'current-status', label: 'Current status' },
  { id: 'finding', label: 'The finding' },
  { id: 'defined', label: 'What the guidance defines' },
  { id: 'absent', label: 'What it leaves open' },
  { id: 'terminology', label: 'A terminology split' },
  { id: 'why-it-matters', label: 'Why this matters' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
  { id: 'protocol-relation', label: 'Relation to nomue Protocol' },
  { id: 'public-evidence', label: 'Public evidence' },
];

const EVIDENCE_BASE =
  'https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-protocol/blob/main/evidence/research/foundation-identity/fnd-1';
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="Research"
  type="Research note"
  title="What three welfare guidance documents define, and what they leave to statistics"
  description="Three animal-welfare guidance documents define exactly when a study must end and what is recorded, but none says whether the measurement it prevented still exists."
  published="September 4, 2026"
  status="Source-bounded research finding — two independent close reviews, not peer reviewed; FND-1 gate open"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        <strong>
          This is a source-bounded research note, not a peer-reviewed paper and not a
          Protocol decision.
        </strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Two independent close reviews inspected the frozen report against the same three
        source documents. Both accepted its direction. They disagreed on four points of
        documentation, and each disagreement was settled against the sources rather than
        by counting reviewers. The wider FND-1 research gate remains open, and no
        animal-welfare or missing-data convention has been selected for nomue Protocol.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="finding">
    <h2>The finding</h2>
    <p>
      An animal study can be required to stop before its planned measurements are taken.
      Three widely used welfare guidance documents say precisely when that must happen,
      what triggers it, what action follows, and what has to be recorded. None of them
      says what becomes of the measurement that was never taken.
    </p>
    <p>
      That silence is not a defect to repair here. It marks where one body of guidance
      ends and statistical reasoning begins. The practical risk is that an analysis reads
      a recorded welfare decision as though it had already answered the data question,
      and so adopts an assumption that no source supplies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="defined">
    <h2>What the guidance defines</h2>
    <p>
      The investigation read the complete text of three documents, each recorded by file
      digest, byte size, and page map before reading. All three describe the end of a
      study as a structure with separable parts: a rule fixed in advance, an event with a
      time, and a graded action.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Document</th>
            <th>What it defines directly</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <p><strong>OECD</strong></p>
              <p>
                Guidance Document on the Recognition, Assessment, and Use of Clinical
                Signs as Humane Endpoints for Experimental Animals Used in Safety
                Evaluation. Environment Directorate, ENV/JM/MONO(2000)7, Series on
                Testing and Assessment No. 19.
              </p>
            </td>
            <td>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  A humane endpoint as the earliest indicator of severe pain, distress,
                  suffering, or impending death (paragraph 9, p. 10).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Three graded responses: humane killing, termination of exposure that
                  may be temporary, or dose reduction (paragraph 10, p. 10; paragraph 62,
                  p. 22).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Impending death defined against the observation schedule: a moribund
                  state, or death expected before the next planned observation
                  (paragraph 12, pp. 10&ndash;11).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Endpoints fixed at planning time in the protocol and standard
                  procedures, observation frequency increased once abnormal signs appear,
                  and the timing of signs documented (paragraph 11, p. 10; paragraph 41,
                  p. 17).
                </li>
                <li>
                  The welfare decision bounded by the scientific objective: pain is
                  treated when treatment does not interfere with the study, and a
                  decision to kill weighs the potential loss of valuable data
                  (paragraph 46, pp. 18&ndash;19; paragraph 42, p. 18).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Separate record classes for an animal found dead and an animal humanely
                  killed (Table 1 note 2, p. 26).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Humanely killed animals counted as dosage-dependent deaths
                  (paragraph 54, p. 21) &mdash; a counting rule, not a statement about an
                  unmeasured value.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <p><strong>CCAC</strong></p>
              <p>
                CCAC guidelines: Identification of scientific endpoints, humane
                intervention points, and cumulative endpoints. Canadian Council on Animal
                Care, March 2022.
              </p>
            </td>
            <td>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  Scientific endpoints, at which the approved live animal use is complete,
                  kept structurally separate from intervention points, where the required
                  action is not necessarily euthanasia (glossary, pp. 38&ndash;39;
                  introduction, p. 4).
                </li>
                <li>
                  A graded ladder of interventions: environmental change, supportive care,
                  antibiotics, pain relief, temporary or permanent removal, then
                  euthanasia (p. 4).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Anticipated intervention points distinguished from unexpected negative
                  outcomes such as unrelated illness or a life-support systems failure
                  (pp. 4&ndash;5).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Pilot studies directed at determining welfare-appropriate endpoints
                  rather than at generating usable scientific data (guideline 2, p. 2).
                </li>
              </ul>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <p><strong>Workman et al.</strong></p>
              <p>
                Guidelines for the welfare and use of animals in cancer research. British
                Journal of Cancer (2010) 102(11), 1555&ndash;1577.
              </p>
            </td>
            <td>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  Humane endpoints developed alongside the requirements for a valid
                  scientific outcome, with earlier endpoints able to increase precision
                  (p. 1569).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Intentional use of death as an endpoint treated as unacceptable
                  (p. 1570).
                </li>
                <li>
                  Quantified, domain-specific triggers: for example 20% weight loss at any
                  time, or 15% maintained for 72 hours against pre-treatment or
                  age-matched vehicle controls, and ulceration persisting beyond 48 hours
                  (Box 5, p. 1571).
                </li>
              </ul>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Across all three, the trigger rule, the event and its time, and the action taken
      are separately recorded facts. Where the documents agree, the agreement was treated
      as independent evidence converging on the same scope, never as a vote.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="absent">
    <h2>What it leaves open</h2>
    <p>
      None of the three defines whether a scheduled measurement that euthanasia or death
      prevented still conceptually exists, and none defines a statistical mechanism for
      how such values go missing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Word-boundary searches over the complete text of all three documents returned no
      occurrence of MCAR, MAR, or MNAR. The one occurrence of the word censor is in the
      CCAC text (p. 27), where the guidance reports a cited suggestion that truncated
      longevity studies can be analyzed with proportional-hazards models on censored
      data. That is a design option attributed to other literature; it defines neither
      existence nor a mechanism. The one occurrence of the word missing in the OECD text
      is in a dental-observation list in an annex (p. 38).
    </p>
    <p>
      The vocabulary of clinical estimands is absent as well. The terms estimand, ICH, E9,
      treatment policy, hypothetical, composite, while-on-treatment, and principal
      stratum returned no hits in any of the three documents. The word intercurrent occurs
      twice, both in an OECD annex, in the ordinary veterinary sense of a concurrent
      infection rather than the clinical-trial sense.
    </p>
    <p>
      How that absence was established is recorded rather than asserted. It rests on
      complete text extraction, a full reading of every definitional, procedural,
      endpoint, monitoring, and record section, inspection of page images where the text
      layer was degraded, and targeted word-boundary searches. It does not rest on a
      verified line-by-line reading of every sentence. The absence is therefore graded one
      step below the direct verification used for the definitions above, and the report
      says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the documents do define next to that gap shows the boundary is real rather
      than accidental. The event and its time are recorded facts distinct from the
      observation stream. An animal found dead and an animal humanely killed produce
      differently sourced final records. An observation lost to a life-support systems
      failure is classed as an unexpected negative outcome rather than an anticipated
      intervention point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="terminology">
    <h2>A terminology split</h2>
    <p>
      The three documents do not share one word for the same idea. The 2022 CCAC
      guidelines deliberately replace humane endpoint with humane intervention point,
      because the required action is not necessarily the end of anything. The OECD and
      Workman documents use humane endpoint throughout.
    </p>
    <p>
      The investigation preserved that split instead of smoothing it. Any later
      vocabulary decision has to face the disagreement as the sources state it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why this matters</h2>
    <p>
      The narrow technical result is that the endpoint, its time, and the action taken are
      directly evidenced as separable declared facts across three separately issued
      documents, from at least two institutionally independent issuing bodies, while the
      existence status of an unmeasured outcome is carried entirely by statistical
      sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wider point concerns how a verification system should treat such a record. A
      statement that an animal reached a humane endpoint is a well-defined and
      well-recorded fact. It is not a statement about whether the measurement that did not
      happen exists, and treating it as one adopts an assumption that no inspected source
      provides. This is the kind of fact a Record has to carry explicitly rather than
      leave an analysis to infer. The finding is research input for that design question.
      It changes no current capability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The conclusions hold for three artifacts at recorded digests, in the specified
      editions and page maps. They do not extend to other renditions or to guidance that
      was not inspected.
    </p>
    <p>
      The originally commissioned 1998 CCAC text could not be obtained, and the 2022 CCAC
      guidelines were inspected in its place. That substitution rests on the same issuing
      body and the same stated role, not on a formal notice of supersession. A claim that
      one document supersedes the other would need an official CCAC record.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two further leads, Morton (2000) and Toth (2000), were not inspected, and no
      conclusion here rests on them. Network routes to the publishers and repositories
      were blocked in the investigating environment, so the three documents were supplied
      directly, and their digests, byte sizes, and page maps were recorded before any
      content was read.
    </p>
    <p>
      This note does not recommend an endpoint, a threshold, a euthanasia rule, or an
      analysis method. It does not rank the three documents, and it establishes no
      missing-data assumption for any study.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol-relation">
    <h2>Relation to nomue Protocol</h2>
    <p>
      The finding is research input for future identity and interpretation design. It adds
      no Record field, vocabulary term, method identifier, schema, public check, supported
      procedure, or implementation behavior, and it selects no animal-welfare or
      missing-data convention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four of eight recorded FND-1 holds are closed in narrowed form. Four holds remain
      open, so the complete FND-1 research gate is still open, and adopting any result
      here would require a separate Protocol proposal and review.
    </p>
  </section>

